--TEST--
Two `Core\Process\Result`s held at once each keep their own run's status and output — the same program started twice with different arguments, and both results read only after both children have exited — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Process::run` builds its result out of the `Output` the wait handed
// back, so every landed case reads a result while it is the only one alive and
// could not tell that apart from a member reading a buffer the *next* run
// overwrites. This case keeps two results alive across each other: both
// children are started and both have exited before either result is asked
// anything, so a member answering out of anything the second run touched
// answers the second run's marker to the first result's question.
//
// The two runs differ in both directions a result carries — a different marker
// on stdout and a different status — because the failure has two shapes and
// only one of them is about bytes. A result sharing a capture fails the marker
// half; a result sharing the status word fails the exit half; each looks
// entirely correct against the other's assertions.
//
// Both halves of each marker are named, as in
// `process-a-completed-runs-two-streams-stay-apart.nvst`: the marker that must
// be there and the other run's marker that must not. `FIRST` and `SECOND` are
// compared as `bytes` and never converted, since what a child writes is not
// this program's to assume anything about (`rule:types/bytes`).
//
// The child is the platform's own shell, named absolutely, for the reason
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` states:
// § 1 makes a `PATH` lookup the caller's own, and the candidate that is not
// this platform's starts nothing — `IOError` on Windows, `RuntimeError` for a
// path with no root on Unix.

// `-2` and not `-1`, again because a signalled child answers `-1`; the byte
// sentinels are non-empty so that a run in which nothing started cannot print
// a plausible line.
bytes $firstOut = "sentinel" as bytes;
bytes $secondOut = "sentinel" as bytes;
int $firstStatus = -2;
int $secondStatus = -2;
int $started = 0;

try {
    var $unixFirst = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "printf FIRST; exit 1"]);
    var $unixSecond = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", "printf SECOND; exit 2"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $firstOut = $unixFirst->stdout();
    $secondOut = $unixSecond->stdout();
    $firstStatus = $unixFirst->exitCode();
    $secondStatus = $unixSecond->exitCode();
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
}

try {
    var $windowsFirst = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "echo FIRST& exit 1"]);
    var $windowsSecond = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "echo SECOND& exit 2"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $firstOut = $windowsFirst->stdout();
    $secondOut = $windowsSecond->stdout();
    $firstStatus = $windowsFirst->exitCode();
    $secondStatus = $windowsSecond->exitCode();
} catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
}

echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "first exit=", $firstStatus, "\n";
echo "second exit=", $secondStatus, "\n";

if (Core\Bytes::contains($firstOut, "FIRST" as bytes)) {
    echo "the first result kept its own output\n";
} else {
    echo "the first result lost its output\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($firstOut, "SECOND" as bytes)) {
    echo "the first result took the second run's output\n";
} else {
    echo "the first result has nothing of the second run\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($secondOut, "SECOND" as bytes)) {
    echo "the second result kept its own output\n";
} else {
    echo "the second result lost its output\n";
}
if (Core\Bytes::contains($secondOut, "FIRST" as bytes)) {
    echo "the second result took the first run's output\n";
} else {
    echo "the second result has nothing of the first run\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned by `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`,
# and this case is about what two completed runs kept apart.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
first exit=1
second exit=2
the first result kept its own output
the first result has nothing of the second run
the second result kept its own output
the second result has nothing of the first run
